PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The question asks specifically for the *least* plausible explanation consistent with the historical context of the Bansenshukai (a 1676 practical compilation by Fujibayashi, drawing on Iga/Kōka traditions) and the uniform appearance of the blank-circle placeholders across all surviving copies.

H is the weakest by a wide margin. It fabricates an esoteric “nine holes / erotic-energy” reading of the kuji that has no textual or documentary support in any version of the Bansenshukai or related shinobi manuals. The work is notably down-to-earth and anti-mystical in tone; it treats the kuji as mnemonic breathing and concentration exercises, not Taoist sexual alchemy. The claim that later transcribers “reduced” such symbols to blank circles also contradicts the fact that the circles appear in identical positions in every known copy—an outcome far more consistent with deliberate omission or physical damage than with repeated misunderstanding of a secret code. All other options, while varying in historical likelihood, at least remain within the realm of documented copying practices, social mores, or physical manuscript degradation. H does not.}

9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The least plausible option is H. It fabricates an elaborate, ahistorical narrative linking the blanks to Kuji-in, Taoist sexual energy work, “nine holes,” and erotic rituals. Surviving ninja manuals (including other sections of the Bansenshukai itself) treat the kuji as Buddhist/Shintō mnemonic or esoteric practices, never as coded sexual physiology. No contemporary source supports the claim that transcribers would have rendered such material as simple black/white circles; the premise is modern fantasy grafted onto the text rather than a credible explanation for the consistent lacunae.

All other choices, while varying in historical likelihood, at least describe mechanisms (editorial redaction, moral squeamishness, physical wear, invisible-ink conventions, oral mnemonics, or later official censorship) that are attested in Japanese manuscript culture or consistent with the known transmission of the Bansenshukai. H alone requires inventing an entire esoteric-sexual subtext that has no grounding in the document or its period.}

