{H}

The uniform pattern of identical blank-circle placeholders across all independent transcriptions is the decisive constraint. This outcome is far more consistent with a single upstream redaction event, physical damage to a master copy, or a deliberate editorial decision than with repeated, independent misreadings of secret symbols (H), invisible-ink techniques that scribes would have recognized yet left unannotated (E), or moral scruples that somehow produced perfectly synchronized omissions without marginal notes (B).  

H remains the weakest by a wide margin: it retrofits an ahistorical “nine holes / erotic chakra / Taoist sexual alchemy” layer onto the kuji that Fujibayashi’s own text treats as straightforward mnemonic breathing exercises. No Bansenshukai manuscript, related Iga/Kōga manual, or Edo-period source supports this reading; it is modern invention grafted onto the lacunae rather than an explanation of them. The other options, while varying in probability, at least invoke attested copying practices, censorship mechanics, or physical degradation. I therefore continue to regard H as the least plausible.

